{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "The body",
      "body": "Spanish usually uses *el / la* with body parts, not \"my\": *Me lavo las manos* (I wash my hands). Who owns the hands is already clear from the pronoun *me*."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What hurts: doler",
      "body": "*Doler* (to hurt) works like *gustar*: the body part is the subject. And it has an o → ue stem change.\n\n- One thing hurts: *Me duele la cabeza.* (My head hurts / I have a headache.)\n- Several things hurt: *Me duelen los pies.* (My feet hurt.)\n- *¿Te duele algo?* (Does anything hurt?) *¿Qué le duele?* (What hurts? — doctor to patient)"
    },
    {
      "heading": "How you feel",
      "body": "Two patterns:\n\n- *estar* + adjective for states: *Estoy cansado, enfermo, nervioso, contento, triste, preocupado* (worried), *enojado* (angry — *enfadado* in Spain), *aburrido* (bored), *mejor* (better).\n- *tener* + noun for sensations: *tengo hambre* (I'm hungry), *tengo sed* (thirsty), *tengo sueño* (sleepy), *tengo frío / calor*, *tengo miedo* (I'm scared), *tengo fiebre* (I have a fever), *tengo gripe* (I have the flu). Intensify with *mucho / mucha*: *Tengo mucha hambre, tengo mucho sueño*.\n\nTo ask: *¿Cómo te sientes?* (How do you feel?) — *Me siento mal* (I feel bad/sick)."
    }
  ],
